13:27:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're pointing at something real—when a group jokes about a stereotype about themselves, that's genuinely different evidence than outsiders making the same joke. It suggests at least a recognized cultural pattern within the field, not just external mockery. I'll grant that fully. But a few things are worth teasing apart before treating it as settled fact: **In-group jokes are partly identity performance, not just description.** Groups often lean into stereotypes about themselves as a bonding ritual—"we're the nerds, and we own it" becomes a badge of belonging, especially in fields that used to be (or still feel) marginalized or mocked. That doesn't mean it's literally true of most individuals; it means the joke is *socially useful* regardless of its accuracy. **Selection into the joke matters.** The engineers who make these jokes loudly are often the same ones who fit the stereotype and enjoy it—confident, funny "nerds." The engineers who don't fit the mold (there are plenty of extroverted, socially smooth engineers) aren't going to volunteer "well actually, I don't think I'm awkward," because there's no reason to contradict a lighthearted joke. So the loud confirmations get remembered; quiet non-confirmations don't register. **"Common knowledge" isn't the same as "well-measured trait."** Common knowledge gave us plenty of confidently wrong stereotypes historically. Popularity of a belief and its accuracy are just different axes—reinforcing each other through repetition doesn't make something more true, just more familiar. **None of this means zero correlation.** I'd bet if you actually measured average self-reported social comfort or "nerdiness" across professions, engineering would skew slightly above the mean on nerd-coded traits compared to, say, sales. That's plausible and consistent with what you're describing. What I'm pushing back on is the jump from "there's a real, joked-about pattern" to "so every individual engineer probably 